    "questionVault": [
      {
        "question": "Explain why civil rights legislation was passed in the years 1964–65. (12 marks)",
        "stimulus1": "Televised protests (Selma/Birmingham)",
        "stimulus2": "President Lyndon B. Johnson",
        "clue": "Explain how media coverage of non-violent campaigns created national outrage, how President Johnson used political maneuverings to overcome Dixiecrats, and how the Selma march forced voting rights legislation.",
        "answer": "“One reason why civil rights legislation was passed in the years 1964–65 was that non-violent direct action campaigns provoked violent responses that shocked the national public.” Campaigns in Birmingham (1963) and Selma (1965) were met with police dogs, water cannons under [[Bull Connor]], and state trooper attacks on [[Bloody Sunday]]. Televised media coverage of this brutality generated massive public sympathy and demand for reform. {{Therefore, the public outrage generated by media coverage of white violence was a primary reason why civil rights legislation was passed in the years 1964–65 because it made federal inaction politically impossible.}}\n\n“A second reason why civil rights legislation was passed in the years 1964–65 was the strong legislative leadership and political skill of President Lyndon B. Johnson.” Johnson used the national tragedy of [[John F. Kennedy's assassination]] to lobby congressmen, passing the [[Civil Rights Act of 1964]]. He broke a record-breaking Southern filibuster and pushed through the [[Voting Rights Act of 1965]] after Selma. {{Therefore, President Johnson's political pressure and commitment were crucial reasons why civil rights legislation was passed in the years 1964–65 because they bypassed Southern obstruction in Congress.}}\n\n“A third reason why civil rights legislation was passed in the years 1964–65 was the growing pressure from mass protests and the threat of civil unrest if voting rights were denied.” The 1963 [[March on Washington]] mobilized 250,000 people, showing widespread support. In Selma, the complete exclusion of Black voters from registrars highlighted the failure of local democracy, forcing the federal government to intervene. {{Therefore, the escalation of mass protests and local voting blockades were vital reasons why civil rights legislation was passed in the years 1964–65 because they forced the federal government to establish national voting safeguards.}}"
      }
    ],
    "summaryCorrection": {
      "text": "The peak of legislative success occurred in 1964–65. SCLC launched the Birmingham Campaign in [[1961 -> 1963]], provoking Police Commissioner Bull Connor to use fire hoses against children. This was followed by the March on Washington, where King gave his famous speech. Orthodox historians argue that these bills were passed primarily due to [[John F. Kennedy's -> Lyndon B. Johnson's]] legislative skill. In contrast, grassroots historians argue that the SCLC's strategy of [[conciliatory negotiations -> deliberate provocation]] of white violence was the true driver. By forcing televised brutality, such as the police attacks at the [[1968 -> 1965]] Selma march, activists shocked the nation's conscience, making it politically impossible for Congress to block the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the [[Fair Housing -> Voting Rights]] Act of 1965."
    },
